InitThread: the main thread's priority after the call (journal 004)

What it fixes: ps2_syscalls::InitThread (ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Syscalls/Thread.cpp) replaces the
game's own InitThread (0x10D078, libkernel's) as a whole, but it only returned the main thread's id.
The function it replaces also ends with ChangeThreadPriority(GetThreadId(), 1) (its decompilation
shows GetThreadId() followed by ChangeThreadPriority(..., 1) after StartThread; the same function
also creates 'SceKerneltopThread' and a semaphore, which this patch does not add). With the main
thread left at the priority 0 the scheduler initialises it with, the game's CRI middleware (0x314FF0)
copies that priority with ReferThreadStatus into the four threads it creates (0x314BE0, 0x314D40,
0x314DC8, 0x314E50), and EeScheduler::createThread rejects priority 0 with KE_ILLEGAL_PRIORITY
(EeScheduler.cpp:426): the four threads were never created and the ADXF file read they drive never
completed, so the boot stayed in the poll loop at 0x331C60 (journal 004). The handler now calls
EeScheduler::changePriority(currentThreadId(), 1, false, old), exactly as the runtime's own
ChangeThreadPriority handler does, and keeps returning EeScheduler::kMainThreadId.

How it was verified: tests/runtime/init_thread_priority_test.cpp (new) runs a host function as the
guest on a real PS2Runtime, on the runtime's own game thread: the guest calls InitThread, reads its
own status the way the game does (GetThreadId + ReferThreadStatus), builds the parameter block the
game builds for its CRI threads with the priority it read, and calls CreateThread and StartThread.
It checks that the priority read is 1, that the thread is created with a positive id and that its
entry function runs after StartThread. Against the tree without this patch the test fails (priority
read 0, CreateThread -403, the entry never runs); with it all checks pass, and the other executables
of tests/runtime (host_gs_hook, host_loop, host_pad_source, host_vu1_hook, iop_host_spu2,
module_buffer_load) pass, built with PS2X_WITH_RAYLIB=OFF and DISPLAY unset. In the game itself
(bash tools/run/smoke.sh 60, headless, the port's host program) a gdb trace shows
ps2_syscalls::InitThread called once from 0x116618 (the game's _InitSys) and the four CreateThread
calls now carrying initial_priority 1, StartThread returning ids 2 to 5. The game no longer stalls
for this reason; the wait at 0x331C60 continues for a later, independent cause (journal 005).

Origin: original work for this project (T-016). The console behaviour is taken from the game's own
code (0x10D078, tools/re/decompile.sh) and from ps2_syscalls::ChangeThreadPriority in the same file.

diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Syscalls/Thread.cpp b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Syscalls/Thread.cpp
index 5110ee2..d1f4dd6 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Syscalls/Thread.cpp
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Syscalls/Thread.cpp
@@ -13,6 +13,10 @@ namespace ps2_syscalls
             return result;
         }
 
+        // The main thread's priority after the console's InitThread: its own code (0x10D078) ends with
+        // ChangeThreadPriority(GetThreadId(), 1). See the handler below and journal 004.
+        constexpr int kInitThreadMainPriority = 1;
+
         int rawThreadStatus(EeThreadStatus status)
         {
             switch (status)
@@ -180,9 +184,20 @@ namespace ps2_syscalls
         setReturnS32(ctx, KE_OK);
     }
 
-    void InitThread(uint8_t *, R5900Context *ctx, PS2Runtime *)
+    void InitThread(uint8_t *rdram, R5900Context *ctx, PS2Runtime *runtime)
     {
+        // This handler replaces the game's own InitThread (0x10D078) as a whole. That function ends
+        // with ChangeThreadPriority(GetThreadId(), 1), so the call has to be made here, the same way
+        // ChangeThreadPriority below does it. The game's CRI middleware (0x314FF0) reads this
+        // thread's priority with ReferThreadStatus and copies it into the four threads it creates
+        // (0x314BE0, 0x314D40, 0x314DC8, 0x314E50). With the main thread left at the priority 0 the
+        // scheduler initialises it with, CreateThread rejects them with KE_ILLEGAL_PRIORITY and the
+        // ADXF read they drive never completes (journal 004).
+        EeScheduler &ee = scheduler(rdram, ctx, runtime);
+        int oldPriority = 0;
+        ee.changePriority(ee.currentThreadId(), kInitThreadMainPriority, false, oldPriority);
         setReturnS32(ctx, EeScheduler::kMainThreadId);
+        ee.transferIfRequested(false);
     }
 
     void CreateThread(uint8_t *rdram, R5900Context *ctx, PS2Runtime *runtime)
